    </div>
    <div class="placer-faq">
      <details open>
        <summary>What is placer mining equipment?</summary>
        <p>Placer mining equipment separates gold from loose alluvial deposits - sand, gravel and clay - rather than hard rock. The core machine is a gold wash plant, which combines a grizzly intake, a trommel or screen, and a recovery sluice into one unit. Supporting gear includes classifiers, highbankers, sluice boxes and concentrators.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What equipment do you need for placer gold mining?</summary>
        <p>Most placer gold operations need three things: a feed source (excavator or loader), a wash plant to screen material and recover gold at 95-98%, and water. A wash plant replaces a separate screen, trommel and sluice box because it does all three jobs in one portable machine.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What is the difference between placer and alluvial gold mining?</summary>
        <p>The terms are used almost interchangeably. Placer gold is any gold found in loose surface deposits; alluvial gold specifically means placer gold deposited by moving water in rivers, streams and old channels. Both are recovered with the same wash-plant-based equipment.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What is the best placer mining equipment for fine gold?</summary>
        <p>Fine gold recovery comes down to sluice design and water control. Our wash plants recover gold down to 200 mesh using a mercury-free sluice, so you keep the flour gold most setups lose. For very fine material, pairing a wash plant with a final-stage concentrator maximizes recovery.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How much does placer mining equipment cost?</summary>
        <p>A gold wash plant is the main investment and is custom-quoted by capacity and configuration, from a portable 50 TPH unit for small claims up to a 300 TPH commercial plant. Because every plant is built to order, pricing is tailored to your operation - <a href="/contact/">request a free quote</a> for current numbers.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

